#backroom %% "behind the watchtower"
(room *)
(name * in #dative) (name #watchtower in #dative) āppa
(name * in $Case) (name #watchtower in $Case)
(* uses preverb #behind)
(short name *) āppa
(dict * in $) āppa āppan
(from * go #south to #gatehouse)
(from * go #up to #roof)
(from * go #north to #enemyland)

(location name *) (name #watchtower in #dative) %% avoid āppa āppa

%% Make sure this doesn't cause ambiguity with GO TO WATCHTOWER
(unlikely [$ pāun $ $ $Dats $])
	(* is one of $Dats)

(prevent [[go #roof] $Details])
	(current room *)
	~(#ladder is #in *)
	(describe failed action [[cross #roof] $Details])
	Nu šuḫḫan parku ēšta. %% "The roof is high up"

(prevent [[go #roof] $Details])
	(current player $Player)
	(#amphora is #heldby $Player)
	(describe failed action [[cross #roof] $Details])
	Nu (#nominative #amphora) nakkiš ēšta. %% "The amphora is heavy."

#roof
(room *)
(neuter *)
(* uses preverb #on)
(name * in #nominative/#accusative) šuḫḫan
(name * in #genitive) šuḫḫaš
(name * in #dative) šuḫḫi
(name * in #ablative) šuḫḫaz
(name * in #bare) šuḫḫa
(from * go #down to #backroom)
(from * go #north to #precinct)

#precinct
(room *)
(* uses preverb #at)
(name * in #nominative/#genitive) gurtaš
(name * in #dative) gurti
(name * in #accusative) gurtan
(name * in #ablative) gurtaz
(name * in #bare) gurtan
(determiner *) E₂
(dict * in $) E2 É

%% For later: "temple" is É^karimmi, neuter: karimmi, karimnac, karimni, karimnaz

(instead of [[go *] $Details])
	(if) (#orb is #heldby #me) (then)
		(describe action [[go *] $Details])
		(div @error) { The tablet breaks off here, as the soldier finally approaches the temple itself. }
		(game over { To be continued... } )
	(else)
		(describe failed action [[go *] $Details])
		Nu (#accusative #orb) natta ḫarkun. %% "I did not have a dammuri-object."
		(div @error) {
			Why exactly the narrator requires a dammuri-object is unclear. Presumably it was so obvious to the ancient scribe that it did not need to be explained; but to modern scholars, it remains a mystery. This is unfortunately quite a common situation in ancient texts.
		}
		(tick) (stop)
	(endif)

#enemyland
(room *)
(* uses preverb #at)
(name * in #nominative/#genitive) ḫappiraš
(name * in #dative/#bare) ḫappiri
(name * in #accusative) ḫappiran
(name * in #ablative) ḫappiraz

(prevent [[go *] | $Details])
	(describe negative action [[go *] | $Details]) %% did not rather than could not
	Nu (#nominative *) kurur ēšta. %% "The city was hostile."

(parse metacommand [\/test watchtower])
	(now) (#ladder is #in #corner)
	(now) (#ladder is handled)
	(now) (#street is visited)
	(now) (#bolt is pristine)
	(now) (#bolt is #in #gate)
	(now) (direction numbers enabled)
	(enter #gatehouse)

(parse metacommand [\/test roof])
	(now) (#bolt is #heldby #me)
	(now) (#bolt is filled with #fire)
	(now) (#amphora is filled with #water)

%% Tutorial!

(tutorial)
	~(given first message)
	(div @tutorial) {
		Welcome to (span @title) { Ḫattušumna Laḫḫa }! This game is presented entirely in the ancient Hittite language, which is probably a bit intimidating at first. But don't panic; just take it slow. (par)
		Clicking a word will show you its definition. See if you can use that to figure out the meaning of the two sentences at the top left. When you think you understand them, press ↩ to continue.
	}
	(now) (given first message)
	(if) ~(using separate panels) (then)
		(now) (update needed) %% Redraw the status bar to include verbs next time
	(endif)
	(now) (map update needed) %% And add the map regardless of separate panels

(tutorial)
	~(taught syntax)
	(div @tutorial) {
		You probably came up with something like "I was at the riverbank. I saw a river there." Most Hittite sentences start with a meaningless "connective" word, then have a series of nouns, and finish off with a verb. The (span @italic) case of those nouns indicates their purpose: a "dative" noun like (span @hittite) wappui indicates a location or destination, while an "accusative" noun like (span @hittite) ḫapan indicates the object of the verb.
		(if) (#watchtower is unvisited) (then)
			(par) Now it's time to construct a sentence of your own. The compass rose at the top right indicates nearby locations, and the panel underneath it suggests some relevant verbs. Double-click any of these words to insert them into your sentence; see if you can use them to move to a new location. \( You can also type on a keyboard, if you prefer, and just leave off the special accent marks. \)
			(now) (prompted movement specifically)
		(endif)
	}
	(now) (taught syntax)

(tutorial)
	~(taught objects)
	(#watchtower is visited)
	(div @tutorial) {
		(if) (prompted movement specifically) (then)
			There we go!
		(endif)
		You'll encounter many more verbs and nouns as you go along, but all the relevant ones will be shown at the top of the screen: what you can see and where you can go. \( You can also leave off the sentence connectives if you want. \)
	}
	(now) (taught objects)

(tutorial)
	~(taught determinatives)
	(#gatehouse is visited)
	(div @tutorial) {
		Hittite nouns were sometimes written with a special sign in front, called a "determiner" or "determinative". These weren't pronounced, but indicated some property of the noun, like how "amber" is a rock but "Amber" is a person. GIŠ, for example, marks that something is made of wood. (par)
		These signs are normally transcribed in superscript, but for typing, you can use a ^ symbol instead \( GIŠ^ḫattalu \) .
	}
	(now) (taught determinatives)

(tutorial)
	~(taught heterograms)
	(heterograms used)
	(div @tutorial) {
		(span @akk) {U₃} is the name of a cuneiform sign that functions like the "&" symbol in English: a shorter way to write the word "and". The capital letters in our transcription show that this is the name of the sign, not the actual Hittite pronunciation.
	}
	(now) (taught heterograms)

(on every tick) %% Make sure this comes BEFORE redrawing the status bar
	~(direction numbers enabled)
	(current room #watchtower)
	{ (#street is visited) (or) (#corner is visited) }
	(now) (direction numbers enabled)

(tutorial)
	~(taught numbers)
	(direction numbers enabled)
	(div @tutorial) {
		You're getting the hang of this now! Since you'll be moving around a lot in this game, there's a shorter way to get from place to place: the Babylonians sometimes numbered their compass directions, 1 south 2 north 3 east 4 west, so you can type "1 3 2" to go south, then east, then north. These numbers will be shown on the compass rose in the top right. (par)
		There's no evidence that the Hittites did this, and this game adds numbers 5 and 6 for "up" and "down", which is completely non-historical. But the Hittites imitating Babylonian writing isn't much of a stretch, and typing a full sentence every time gets tedious very fast! You can also click the boxes on the map to navigate to anywhere you've been before.
	}
	(now) (taught numbers)

(tutorial)
	~(taught multiple nouns)
	(#bolt is handled)
	(#foyer is visited)
	(div @tutorial) {
		Most of the sentences you've seen so far have only one noun, but this doesn't have to be the case. To put one thing into or onto another, for example, you'll need both an accusative noun (span @italic) and a dative noun.
	}
	(now) (taught multiple nouns)

(avoid genitive case) %% For this first tablet specifically
(avoid ablative case)

%% And finally, the circumstances in which each verb gets unlocked
(verb #v-look unlocked)
(verb #v-go unlocked)
(verb #v-cross unlocked) (#watchtower is visited)
(verb #v-take unlocked) (#street is visited)
(verb #v-drop unlocked) (#ladder is handled)
(verb #v-put unlocked) (#gatehouse is visited)
(verb #v-slay unlocked) (#foyer is visited)
